WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.140
Tesla enthusiasts may be eagerly anticipating
the first Cybertrucks or Semis to roll off

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.880
the assembly line, but according to Elon Musk,
there is something even more important coming

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.880
down the pike.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.160
It’s hard to imagine that a company known
for electric vehicles could one day be more

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.670
famous for a robot.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:23.390
This, I think, has the potential to be more
significant than the vehicle business over

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.390
time.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.119
Why on earth would Musk make such a bold statement?

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:28.119
Because he sees the potential.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.149
The market for labor is much bigger 
than the market for cars.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:37.329
The $45 trillion-dollar global labor market
is 16 times bigger than the global car and

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:40.859
manufacturing market, estimated to be about
$3 trillion.

00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:45.780
Optimus came as a surprise during Tesla’s
artificial intelligence event in August.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.350
Except they used a dancing human in a robot
suit to show it off since there wasn’t - and

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.280
still isn’t - a prototype.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.359
Some media outlets dismissed the Tesla bot
as a “joke” at the time.

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:58.210
But if you ask Musk, he’s serious about it.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.300
The Tesla bot WILL be real.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.180
Musk said it makes sense to develop robots
because it already has the best and brightest

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.200
engineers working on similar technology for
its cars.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.950
Its electric vehicles are learning how to
navigate the world using cameras and neural

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:11.950
networks.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.560
Just like the cars, the robot would use the
autopilot’s cameras to “see” and be

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.000
equipped with a full self-driving computer.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.210
Instead of a face, it’ll have a screen.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.810
But other than that, it resembles an average-sized
human, at 5’8” and 125-pounds.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.310
Initially, the Tesla bot is meant to help
out with everyday tasks.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.470
It should be able to, y’know, ‘Pease go
to the store and get me the following groceries’.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.680
Musk anticipates the robot will also be used
in Tesla’s factories.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.980
If we can't find a use for it, then we shouldn’t
expect that others would.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:50.670
The first use of the Tesla Optimus robot would
be at Tesla moving parts around the factory

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:51.670
or something like that.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:52.939
That seems like a simple task.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:57.700
However, as the technology improves, robots
have the potential to eliminate all boring

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.039
and repetitive jobs.

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:01.109
They’re already frying in some restaurants.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:06.340
And one day, could do dangerous work like
exploring mines, inspecting water pipes, and

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:09.950
could be employed in fulfillment centers as
e-commerce continues to grow.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:15.290
And who knows - they could even help SpaceX
build a city on Mars.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.050
Robots are highly productive - they don’t
take breaks.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.050
They work all the time.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.720
And they don’t get paid.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.660
For those worried about losing their jobs,
robots could help solve the labor shortage

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.150
hitting the U.S. hard to such a point that
McDonald’s has had to cut back their hours

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.020
at thousands of stores.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:36.440
Nearly nine million Americans called in sick
in January due to Omicron - exasperating problems

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:42.830
already caused by last year’s “great resignation”
where people quit their jobs at unprecedented levels.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:47.020
What happens when there’s 
no shortage of labor?

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.590
Some predict we could get to the point where
humans won’t even have to work unless they

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:51.900
really wanted to.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.540
Billionaires like Musk, Richard Branson, and
Mark Zuckerberg are backing a universal basic

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.020
income for citizens.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.590
Tesla has been beefing up its AI team significantly.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:05.800
The head of the division, Andrej Karpathy,
is hiring deep learning software engineers

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.540
to help leverage their expertise beyond their
vehicle fleet.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.640
But what about Musk’s own declared fear of robots?

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:19.370
He’s warned of scary outcomes like the Terminator
potentially wiping out human civilization.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.030
He once commented on a viral video of a Boston
Dynamics robot performing parkour by tweeting:

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.830
“In a few years, that bot will move so fast
you’ll need a strobe light to see it.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.950
Sweet dreams.”

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.121
Perhaps Musk figures it’s better to build
robots his way than have someone else to

00:03:34.121 --> 00:03:35.580
build them their way.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:41.530
When YouTuber and investor Dave Lee surmised Musk is 
taking matters into his own hands to “save humanity from

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.430
evil AI robots” - he got a like from the
Tesla CEO.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.790
Musk may have had this in mind for years.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:52.180
In 2015, he co-founded the non-profit research
firm OpenAI.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.950
He later left its board to avoid conflicts
of interest with Tesla.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:02.540
OpenAI has the explicit goal of ensuring “artificial
general intelligence benefits all of humanity”.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.320
Artificial general intelligence is different
from artificial intelligence.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.120
AI is capable of the tasks it’s been trained
to do.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.540
An AGI system can do almost anything humans
can do without any oversight from us.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.700
In other words, it is truly intelligent.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.989
We haven’t yet achieved artificial general
intelligence.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:23.259
Some question whether this is even possible.

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:28.009
But when a computer beats the best chess player
in the world or the best AlphaGo player in

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:32.389
the world, and when deep neural networks can
detect a person’s sexual orientation by

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.699
studying their face,

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:40.620
the AI research community believes the exponential
advancement of AI could culminate into AGI.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:45.000
To prevent a Terminator-like scenario, Musk
has stressed he plans on decentralizing the

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.120
control of Tesla bots.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.770
In its current form, the robot appears less
intimidating than the ones created by Hollywood.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.400
It’s intended to be friendly, of course.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:03.964
We’re setting it such that it is at a mechanical level, at a physical 
level, you can run away from it

00:05:03.964 --> 00:05:06.009
 and most likely overpower it.

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:10.740
Musk has said Optimus could be in production
by the end of 2023 and previously stated a

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.490
prototype could be unveiled in 2022.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.220
Although deadlines are not his strong suit.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.860
Tesla is also juggling plenty on its plate.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.960
Production of the Cybertruck has been delayed
until 2023 and Musk confirmed the company

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.999
is not currently working on a less expensive
$25,000 car it had previously mentioned.

00:05:29.999 --> 00:05:35.129
Just like cars, the difficulty lies not only
in building robots but in mass producing them.

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:39.479
It could be years before the bot becomes a
core part of Tesla’s business let alone

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:41.360
overtake sales in vehicles.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.590
There is one sector a humanoid robot
won’t overtake.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.590
Driving.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.599
Instead, Musk is betting on robotaxis.

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:53.949
A network of fully autonomous Teslas that
don’t even need a steering wheel.

00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:59.180
Regardless of whether it’s a robot or a
robotaxi, robotics in one form or another

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.800
will be driving Tesla’s future.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.940
Some of the best engineering minds in the
world at Tesla are working to solve complex

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:06.940
problems.

